# Git Commands: Practical Reference

Git has many commands, including low-level plumbing commands. This guide covers
the commands used most often in everyday development. To inspect every command
available in your Git installation, run `git help -a`; for conceptual guides,
run `git help -g`.

## 1. Getting Help

```bash
git help <command>
git <command> --help
git <command> -h
git help -a
git help -g
```

- `git help <command>` opens the full manual page for a command, such as
	`git help commit`.
- `git <command> --help` opens that command's manual.
- `git <command> -h` prints a short usage summary in the terminal.
- `git help -a` lists available Git commands, including installed extensions.
- `git help -g` lists Git's guides, such as revisions and attributes.

## 2. First-Time Setup

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
git config --global init.defaultBranch main
git config --global core.editor "code --wait"
git config --list --show-origin
```

- `git config` reads or changes configuration. `--global` applies to your user
	account; omit it to set a value only in the current repository. `--system`
	applies machine-wide.
- `user.name` and `user.email` are recorded as the author identity on new
	commits. They do not authenticate you to a hosting service.
- `init.defaultBranch` chooses the initial branch name for new repositories.
- `core.editor` selects the editor Git opens for commit messages and other text.
- `git config --list --show-origin` displays settings and the files they came
	from. Read one value with `git config user.name`; remove it with
	`git config --global --unset user.name`.

Aliases can shorten frequent commands:

```bash
git config --global alias.st "status --short --branch"
git config --global alias.lg "log --oneline --graph --decorate --all"
git st
git lg
```

## 3. Create or Copy a Repository

```bash
git init
git init <directory>
git clone <url>
git clone <url> <directory>
git clone --branch <branch> <url>
```

- `git init` starts a repository in the current directory by creating `.git`.
	It does not add or commit files.
- `git init <directory>` creates a repository in a new or existing directory.
- `git clone <url>` downloads a repository, its history, and normally checks
	out its default branch. The second form chooses the local directory name.
- `--branch` checks out a named branch (or tag) after cloning.

## 4. Check Repository State and Changes

```bash
git status
git status --short
git status --branch
git diff
git diff --staged
git diff HEAD
git diff -- <file>
git diff <commit-a> <commit-b>
```

- `git status` reports the current branch and untracked, modified, staged, or
	conflicted files. It is read-only; run it often.
- `git status --short` gives a compact status. Its two status columns describe
	the index (staging area) and working tree, respectively.
- `git diff` shows unstaged changes compared with the index.
- `git diff --staged` (also `--cached`) shows staged changes compared with the
	latest commit.
- `git diff HEAD` compares tracked working-tree and staged changes with the
	latest commit.
- Add `-- <file>` to limit a diff to a path. `--` separates options from paths.
- `git diff <commit-a> <commit-b>` compares two commits, showing changes from
	the first commit to the second.

## 5. Stage and Commit Changes

```bash
git add <file>
git add <file1> <file2>
git add .
git add -p
git reset <file>
git restore --staged <file>
git commit -m "Describe the change"
git commit
git commit -am "Describe tracked-file changes"
git commit --amend
```

- `git add <file>` stages that file's current content for the next commit.
	Staging specific files helps keep commits focused.
- `git add .` stages new and changed files under the current directory. Check
	`git status` first so unrelated work is not included.
- `git add -p` interactively stages selected parts (hunks) of changes.
- `git reset <file>` and `git restore --staged <file>` unstage a file while
	preserving its working-tree edits.
- `git commit -m "..."` creates a commit from staged changes. A commit is a
	saved snapshot with a message and parent history. `git commit` opens the
	configured editor for the message.
- `git commit -am "..."` stages and commits modifications and deletions to
	already tracked files. It does not include new, untracked files.
- `git commit --amend` replaces the latest commit with a new one, including
	staged changes. It changes history; avoid amending commits already shared with
	others unless your team agrees.

Typical small change:

```bash
git status
git diff
git add src/utils.js
git diff --staged
git commit -m "Add utility function"
git status
```

## 6. Branches and Switching

```bash
git branch
git branch --all
git branch <branch>
git branch -d <branch>
git branch -D <branch>
git switch <branch>
git switch -c <new-branch>
git checkout <branch>
git checkout -b <new-branch>
```

- `git branch` lists local branches; `git branch --all` also lists remote
	tracking branches.
- `git branch <branch>` creates a branch but leaves you on the current branch.
- `git switch <branch>` changes the current branch. Git may stop if switching
	would overwrite uncommitted changes.
- `git switch -c <new-branch>` creates and switches to a branch in one step.
- `git checkout` is the older, multi-purpose command. `switch` is clearer for
	branch operations; `restore` is clearer for restoring files.
- `git branch -d <branch>` deletes a branch only if Git considers it merged.
- `git branch -D <branch>` force-deletes a local branch, including unmerged
	work. Use it only when you are sure those commits are no longer needed.
- Names such as `feature/add-search` and `fix/config-error` make intent clear.

## 7. Merge and Rebase

```bash
git merge <branch>
git merge --no-ff <branch>
git merge --abort
git rebase <base-branch>
git rebase --continue
git rebase --abort
```

- `git merge <branch>` incorporates the named branch into the current branch.
	Switch to the destination branch first. Git may create a merge commit or
	fast-forward the branch pointer.
- `git merge --no-ff <branch>` always creates a merge commit, preserving a
	visible branch boundary.
- `git merge --abort` stops a conflicted merge and attempts to return to the
	pre-merge state. Save unrelated work before starting a merge.
- `git rebase <base-branch>` replays the current branch's commits on top of the
	base branch, creating new commit IDs. It can make local history linear.
- During a rebase conflict, resolve and stage files, then use
	`git rebase --continue`. Use `git rebase --abort` to stop and return to the
	pre-rebase state.
- Do not rebase commits others already use unless your team coordinates
	rewriting that shared history.

Feature-branch flow:

```bash
git switch main
git pull --ff-only
git switch -c feature/my-change
# edit files
git add <files>
git commit -m "Implement my change"
git push -u origin feature/my-change
```

## 8. Remotes and Synchronization

```bash
git remote -v
git remote add <name> <url>
git remote rename <old-name> <new-name>
git remote remove <name>
git fetch
git fetch <remote>
git pull
git pull --ff-only
git push
git push -u <remote> <branch>
git push <remote> --delete <branch>
```

- A remote is a named URL for another copy of a repository. `origin` is the
	conventional name for the repository you cloned.
- `git remote -v` shows remote names and their fetch/push URLs. `git remote add`
	registers another remote, commonly an upstream project.
- `git remote remove <name>` removes the local remote configuration; it does
	not delete the hosted repository.
- `git fetch` downloads remote commits and updates remote-tracking references.
	It does not integrate them into your current branch or change working files.
- `git pull` fetches and then integrates the upstream branch, usually by merge
	unless configured otherwise. Inspect your status first.
- `git pull --ff-only` refuses to create a merge commit if a fast-forward is not
	possible.
- `git push` sends local commits to a remote. The first push of a new branch is
	commonly `git push -u origin <branch>`; `-u` sets its upstream so later push
	and pull commands can omit remote and branch names.
- `git push origin --delete <branch>` deletes a branch from the remote, not
	locally. If a push is rejected because the remote has newer commits, fetch
	and integrate them before retrying. Avoid force-pushing shared branches.

## 9. Inspect History

```bash
git log
git log --oneline
git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all
git log -p
git log -- <file>
git show <commit>
git show --stat <commit>
git blame <file>
git shortlog
git shortlog -sn
```

- `git log` lists commits reachable from the current branch, newest first.
- `--oneline` shows a short commit ID and subject. `--graph --decorate --all`
	draws branch relationships and includes all references.
- `git log -p` includes each commit's patch. Add `-- <file>` to show commits
	that changed a particular path.
- `git show <commit>` displays commit metadata and changes. `HEAD` means the
	current commit; `HEAD~1` means its first parent.
- `git show --stat <commit>` summarizes changed files and line counts.
- `git blame <file>` shows the commit and author associated with each line. It
	helps locate context but does not explain why a line changed.
- `git shortlog` groups commits by author; `-sn` shows a sorted count.

## 10. Stash Temporary Work

```bash
git stash
git stash push -m "description"
git stash push -u -m "include new files"
git stash list
git stash show -p stash@{0}
git stash apply
git stash apply stash@{1}
git stash pop
git stash drop stash@{0}
git stash clear
```

- `git stash` saves eligible uncommitted tracked changes and cleans them from
	the working tree. New untracked files are not included by default.
- `git stash push -m "..."` labels the saved work; `-u` also includes
	untracked files.
- `git stash list` shows saved stashes; `stash@{0}` is the newest.
- `git stash show -p` displays a stash's patch.
- `git stash apply` restores a stash and keeps it in the list. `git stash pop`
	restores the newest stash and removes it if applying succeeds.
- `git stash drop` removes one stash. `git stash clear` removes all stashes and
	is difficult to undo; inspect the list before using it.

## 11. Undo and Recover Changes

Choose an undo command based on where the change is: working tree, staging
area, or commit history. These operations are not interchangeable.

```bash
git restore <file>
git restore --staged <file>
git restore --source=<commit> <file>
git revert <commit>
git reset --soft <commit>
git reset --mixed <commit>
git reset --hard <commit>
git reflog
```

- `git restore <file>` discards unstaged working-tree changes to that tracked
	file, restoring it from the index. Discarded edits may be difficult or
	impossible to recover.
- `git restore --staged <file>` unstages a file while keeping its edits in the
	working tree.
- `git restore --source=<commit> <file>` replaces the file with its version at
	that commit. Review the diff before committing the result.
- `git revert <commit>` creates a new commit that reverses the selected commit.
	It is generally safer for undoing a commit that has already been shared.
- `git reset --soft <commit>` moves the current branch to a commit and keeps
	changes staged.
- `git reset --mixed <commit>` (the default reset mode) moves the branch and
	keeps changes in the working tree, unstaged.
- `git reset --hard <commit>` moves the branch and discards tracked staged and
	working-tree changes. It can permanently destroy work; confirm the target and
	check status first. It does not remove untracked files.
- `git reflog` lists recent movements of local references, including many
	commits no longer visible in normal history. To recover one, create a branch:
	`git switch -c recovery <commit-id>`.
- Avoid rewriting commits already pushed to a shared branch. Coordinate with
	collaborators before any history rewrite.

## 12. Resolve Merge Conflicts

1. Run `git status` to see which files are conflicted.
2. Open each file and find conflict markers: `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, and `>>>>>>>`.
3. Edit the file to the intended final content and remove all conflict markers.
4. Review the result, run relevant tests, and stage the resolved file with
	 `git add <file>`.
5. Finish with `git commit` for a merge, or `git rebase --continue` for a rebase.

To cancel, use `git merge --abort` or `git rebase --abort`, as appropriate. Do
not stage a conflicted file until you have resolved and reviewed it.

## 13. Tags and Releases

```bash
git tag
git tag <tag-name>
git tag -a <tag-name> -m "Release message"
git show <tag-name>
git push origin <tag-name>
git push origin --tags
git tag -d <tag-name>
git push origin --delete <tag-name>
```

- `git tag` lists local tags. `git tag <tag-name>` creates a lightweight tag at
	the current commit.
- `git tag -a` creates an annotated tag with a message; annotated tags are
	commonly used for releases.
- `git push` does not automatically publish tags. Push one tag by name or use
	`--tags` to publish all local tags.
- `git tag -d` deletes a local tag. Deleting a remote tag is separate and
	affects everyone using that remote; coordinate first.

## 14. Ignore Files and Clean Up

```bash
git check-ignore -v <path>
git clean -n
git clean -f
git clean -nd
git clean -fd
```

- `.gitignore` lists untracked files and patterns Git should ignore. It does
	not stop tracking a file that has already been committed.
- `git check-ignore -v <path>` explains which ignore rule matches a path.
- `git clean -n` previews untracked files that would be removed. Add `-d` to
	include untracked directories.
- `git clean -f` deletes untracked files; `git clean -fd` also deletes
	untracked directories. These deletions are not normally recoverable. Preview
	with `-n` first. Ignored files are excluded unless `-x` is used;
	`git clean -fdx` can delete build outputs and local data, so use extreme care.

## 15. Search and Compare

```bash
git grep "search text"
git grep -n "search text" <commit>
git diff --check
git diff --word-diff
git log --grep="message text"
git log -S"text" -- <file>
```

- `git grep` searches tracked files in the working tree; `-n` includes line
	numbers. Supplying a commit searches that snapshot instead.
- `git diff --check` finds whitespace errors such as trailing spaces in changes.
- `git diff --word-diff` emphasizes changed words instead of whole lines.
- `git log --grep` finds commit messages matching text.
- `git log -S"text"` finds commits where the number of occurrences of text
	changed, useful for locating when a line or symbol was introduced or removed.

## 16. Submodules and Worktrees

```bash
git submodule status
git submodule update --init --recursive
git worktree list
git worktree add <directory> <branch>
git worktree remove <directory>
```

- Submodules are repositories referenced by another repository. After cloning
	a project that uses them, `git submodule update --init --recursive`
	initializes and checks out the recorded versions.
- A worktree checks out another branch into a separate directory while sharing
	repository data, allowing work on two branches at once.
- Save work inside a worktree before removing it. Git may refuse to remove a
	worktree that contains changes.

## 17. Useful Reference Names

- `HEAD`: the current commit.
- `HEAD~1`: the first parent of the current commit; `HEAD~2` is two first-parent
	commits back.
- `HEAD^`: the first parent; `HEAD^2` means the second parent of a merge commit.
- `<branch>..<other-branch>`: commits reachable from `other-branch` but not from
	`<branch>` when used with commands such as `git log`.
- `@{upstream}`: the upstream branch configured for the current branch.
- `--`: marks the end of options and the beginning of path arguments.

## 18. Safe Habits

- Run `git status` before and after operations that change repository state.
- Review `git diff` before staging and `git diff --staged` before committing.
- Prefer `git revert` for undoing changes that have already been shared.
- Preview cleanup with `git clean -n`; be especially cautious with
	`git reset --hard`, `git clean -f`, and force-pushing.
- Make small commits with clear messages, and avoid committing secrets or local
	credentials.
- When unsure about a command, inspect its short usage with `git <command> -h`
	or its manual with `git help <command>`.
